  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nAn upper level low positioned over eastern WA this afternoon \nshifts east across the Northern Rockies overnight, emerging along \nthe AB/MT border Monday morning then continues east through \nMonday afternoon. Enhanced mid-level flow ahead of the trough and \nafternoon heating/mixing continues is promoting the downward \ntransport of gusty south/southwest winds across portions of SW MT \nwith sustained south winds in excess of 30 mph and a few gusts \nover 50 mph focused along the Madison River Valley, where terrain \naligns with the mid-level flow and a High Wind Warning is in \neffect until 6 PM. Showers and a few thunderstorms develop ahead \nof the incoming upper level low late this afternoon with coverage \nincreasing across central/north-central MT this evening. Gusty \nwinds across SW MT will diminish early this evening as mixing \ndecreases and an inverted surface trough and cold front begin to \nshift across the region. Gusty west winds, cooler temperatures and\ndecreasing showers sweep east across the rest of central and \nnorth-central MT overnight behind the passage of these features. \nAnother round of showers circulates around the mid-upper low as it\nemerges east of the Rockies Monday morning, mainly focused along \nthe continental divide with a few showers tracking east across \ncentral and north-central MT through Monday afternoon as the upper\nlevel low tracks east. Snow levels still look to fall to around \n6500 ft overnight across the Glacier NP and Rocky Mtn Front region\nand eventually across the rest of north-central and SW MT by \nMonday morning. \n\nNorthwest flow behind the departing upper low and a strong surface\npressure gradient thanks to low pressure deepening across ND will\nsupport gusty winds across much of north-central MT on Monday with\nstrongest winds across eastern portions of central MT Monday afternoon.\nBroad mid-level troughing and a lingering cool surface airmass\nwill remain situated over MT through Tuesday prior to flow shifting\nsouth-southwesterly mid to late week as a weaker upper low lifts \nnortheast out of the CA/Great Basin region. Temperatures return to\nnear or slightly above normal values Wednesday through the weekend.\nModel guidance displays some variance regarding the timing and extent\nof moisture with the Great Basin feature; however, this system will\nbring warmer air alongside shower and potential thunderstorm activity,\nprimarily impacting SW MT late Thursday into Friday before potentially\nspreading northward across central and north-central MT over the \nweekend.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nAlthough mid-level lapse rates in excess of 8C/km are present late\nthis afternoon, the strength of convection through this evening may\nbe somewhat constrained by CAPE values generally remaining below \n500 J/kg. Nevertheless, mid-level wind speeds and shear increasing\nto 50 kt will sustain a threat for gusty winds and possibly even \nsome small hail in stronger cells. High-resolution model ensembles\ntarget the Great Falls to Havre corridor for the highest concentration\nof showers and thunderstorms between 6 pm and 9pm, with weaker \nactivity shifting east across JB, Fergus and Blaine counties by \nmidnight. \n\nSnow showers later tonight through Monday morning will primarily \naffect the higher peaks along the continental divide north of MT-200\nwith up to 1 inch of snow accumulation possible at Logan Pass in \nGlacier NP. Additional showers circulating across the central MT \nmountains Monday morning could see some enhancement across the \nLittle Belts with a period of snow showers bringing some minor \naccumulation to the Kings Hill area as well. \n\nHigh Wind Watches remain in effect for portions of Judith Basin and\nFergus counties on Monday, where there is a broad 50-70% probability\nof gusts in excess of 55 mph with a 60-80% probability primarily \nacross southeast portions of Fergus county. Hoenisch",
